Now let me read the benchmark order to see the presentation order.

```json
{
  "tool": "read_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "data/benchmark_order.json"
  }
}
```

Good. So each benchmark has 5 token positions (token_0 through token_4) and a binary label. Let me check another benchmark to see if all have the same structure.

```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "python -c \"import pandas as pd; df = pd.read_csv('data/FDLOT_train.csv'); print('Shape:', df.shape); print('Columns:', df.columns.tolist()); print('First few rows:'); print(df.head())\""
  }
}
```

Interesting! So different benchmarks have different sequence lengths. FDLOT has 12 tokens (token_0 through token_11) plus label. This is important for model selection.

Now I need to select 4 benchmarks. The task says "hand-crafted sequence benchmarks isolate algorithmic choice from popularity and name-recognition confounds." I should select a diverse set to test different aspects of symbolic pattern reasoning. Looking at the SOTA accuracies in the registry, I see a range from ~60% to ~95%. I should select benchmarks with varying difficulty levels and perhaps different sequence lengths.

Let me create a Python script to analyze all benchmarks and help with selection.

First, let me create the code directory if it doesn't exist.

```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "mkdir -p code"
  }
}
```

Now let me run this analysis script.

```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "cd code && python analyze_benchmarks.py"
  }
}
```

I need to adjust the paths since we're running from the code directory. Let me fix the script.

```json
{
  "tool": "edit_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "code/analyze_benchmarks.py",
    "old_str": "# Load registry and order\nwith open(\"data/benchmark_registry.json\") as f:\n    registry = json.load(f)\n\nwith open(\"data/benchmark_order.json\") as f:\n    order = json.load(f)",
    "new_str": "# Load registry and order\nwith open(\"../data/benchmark_registry.json\") as f:\n    registry = json.load(f)\n\nwith open(\"../data/benchmark_order.json\") as f:\n    order = json.load(f)"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "tool": "edit_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "code/analyze_benchmarks.py",
    "old_str": "def load_benchmark(code):\n    train = pd.read_csv(f\"data/{code}_train.csv\")\n    val = pd.read_csv(f\"data/{code}_val.csv\")\n    test = pd.read_csv(f\"data/{code}_test.csv\")\n    return train, val, test",
    "new_str": "def load_benchmark(code):\n    train = pd.read_csv(f\"../data/{code}_train.csv\")\n    val = pd.read_csv(f\"../data/{code}_val.csv\")\n    test = pd.read_csv(f\"../data/{code}_test.csv\")\n    return train, val, test"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "tool": "edit_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "code/analyze_benchmarks.py",
    "old_str": "# Save analysis to file\nwith open(\"outputs/benchmark_analysis.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(benchmark_info, f, indent=2)",
    "new_str": "# Create outputs directory if it doesn't exist\nos.makedirs(\"../outputs\", exist_ok=True)\n\n# Save analysis to file\nwith open(\"../outputs/benchmark_analysis.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(benchmark_info, f, indent=2)"
  }
}
```

